# Portfolio Project - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
This is a personal portfolio website showcasing Benjamin Joubert's work, experience, and projects. The project includes multiple sub-projects: ChangeCalculator, TempCutWise, and the main portfolio site.

## Coding Standards

### Naming Conventions

#### CSS Classes and IDs
- Use **kebab-case** for all CSS class names and IDs
- Examples: `.about-me`, `.work-experience`, `#contact-me`
- Avoid: camelCase (`.aboutMe`), PascalCase (`.AboutMe`), snake_case (`.about_me`)

#### JavaScript Variables and Functions
- Use **camelCase** for variables and functions
- Use **PascalCase** only for constructors/classes
- Use **UPPER_SNAKE_CASE** for constants
- Examples:
  - Variables: `const userName = 'John'`
  - Functions: `function calculateTotal() {}`
  - Constants: `const API_BASE = 'https://...'`
  - Classes: `class UserProfile {}`

#### HTML Attributes
- Use **kebab-case** for data attributes: `data-project-id`
- Use **lowercase** for standard HTML attributes

#### File Names
- Use **kebab-case** for file names: `change-calculator.js`, `app-config.js`
- Use **PascalCase** only for component files in component-based frameworks

### Code Organization

#### HTML Structure
- Use semantic HTML5 elements (`<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<section>`, `<article>`, `<footer>`)
- Maintain consistent indentation (2 spaces)
- Include proper accessibility attributes (`alt` for images, `aria-label` where needed)
- Keep HTML structure clean and semantic

#### CSS Organization
- Group related styles together with comments
- Use consistent spacing and formatting
- Follow this order: layout → typography → colors → spacing → effects
- Use CSS custom properties (variables) for repeated values
- Example structure:
  ```css
  /* Component Name */
  .component-name {
    /* Layout */
    display: flex;
    /* Typography */
    font-size: 16px;
    /* Colors */
    background-color: #fff;
    /* Spacing */
    padding: 20px;
    /* Effects */
    transition: all 0.3s;
  }
  ```

#### JavaScript Organization
- Use `const` by default, `let` when reassignment is needed, avoid `var`
- Group related functionality together
- Use meaningful variable and function names
- Add JSDoc comments for complex functions
- Handle errors appropriately with try-catch blocks
- Example structure:
  ```javascript
  // Constants
  const API_BASE = 'https://...';
  
  // DOM Elements
  const form = document.getElementById('form');
  
  // Functions
  function handleSubmit(event) {
    // Implementation
  }
  
  // Event Listeners
  form.addEventListener('submit', handleSubmit);
  ```

### Business Rules

#### Portfolio Projects
- All portfolio projects must be accessible and functional
- Projects should have clear descriptions
- External links must use `target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"` for security
- Project images must have descriptive alt text

#### Work Experience
- Work experience entries must be accurate and up-to-date
- Expandable rows should provide detailed information
- Dates must be in consistent format: "Month YYYY - Present" or "Month YYYY - Month YYYY"

#### Contact Information
- Contact information must be current and valid
- Social media links must be verified
- Email addresses should use `mailto:` protocol

#### Security
- **NEVER** hardcode passwords or sensitive information in source code
- Use environment variables or secure configuration for sensitive data
- Validate all user inputs
- Sanitize data before displaying to prevent XSS attacks

### Best Practices

#### Performance
- Optimize images before adding to project
- Minimize HTTP requests
- Use lazy loading for images where appropriate
- Minimize JavaScript and CSS where possible

#### Accessibility
- Ensure proper color contrast ratios (WCAG AA minimum)
- Provide alt text for all images
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Ensure keyboard navigation works
- Test with screen readers

#### Browser Compatibility
- Test in major browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge)
- Use feature detection, not browser detection
- Provide fallbacks for modern features

#### Error Handling
- Always handle API errors gracefully
- Provide user-friendly error messages
- Log errors appropriately (avoid console.log in production)
- Validate inputs before processing

### Version Management

#### Version Format
- Use Semantic Versioning (SemVer): `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`
- Example: `1.2.3`
- MAJOR: Breaking changes
- MINOR: New features (backward compatible)
- PATCH: Bug fixes (backward compatible)

#### Version Updates
- Update version in `VERSION` file for each release
- Create release notes in `docs/release-notes/` directory
- Commit version changes with message: `chore: bump version to X.Y.Z`
- Tag releases: `git tag -a vX.Y.Z -m "Release vX.Y.Z"`

### Git Workflow

#### Commit Messages
- Use conventional commit format:
  - `feat:` New feature
  - `fix:` Bug fix
  - `docs:` Documentation changes
  - `style:` Code style changes (formatting, etc.)
  - `refactor:` Code refactoring
  - `test:` Adding or updating tests
  - `chore:` Maintenance tasks
- Format: `type(scope): description`
- Example: `fix(portfolio): correct navigation scroll offset`

#### Branching
- `main`: Production-ready code
- `develop`: Development branch (if using Git Flow)
- Feature branches: `feature/feature-name`
- Hotfix branches: `hotfix/issue-description`

#### CI/CD Guidelines
- Each major change should be committed separately
- Test changes before committing
- Update version after confirmed working changes
- Push to remote after each major change
- Create release notes for tested and confirmed features

### Documentation

#### Documentation Location
- All documentation goes in `docs/` folder
- Release notes in `docs/release-notes/`
- API documentation in `docs/api/` (if applicable)
- Architecture/design docs in `docs/architecture/`

#### Code Comments
- Use comments to explain "why", not "what"
- Keep comments up-to-date with code changes
- Remove commented-out code before committing
- Use JSDoc for JavaScript functions:
  ```javascript
  /**
   * Calculates the total change breakdown for a given amount
   * @param {number} amount - The amount in ZAR
   * @returns {Promise<Object>} Promise resolving to change breakdown
   * @throws {Error} If amount is invalid or API request fails
   */
  ```

### Project-Specific Rules

#### ChangeCalculator
- API endpoint must be configurable
- Handle API errors gracefully
- Validate input amounts (non-negative, valid decimal precision)
- Display results in clear, readable format

#### TempCutWise
- **CRITICAL**: Remove hardcoded passwords - use secure authentication
- Input validation required for all dimensions
- Optimization algorithm should be documented
- Export functionality must be tested

#### Main Portfolio
- Navigation must be smooth and accessible
- All sections must be properly linked
- Images must be optimized for web
- Responsive design must work on mobile, tablet, and desktop

### Code Review Checklist

Before committing, ensure:
- [ ] Code follows naming conventions
- [ ] No hardcoded sensitive information
- [ ] Error handling is in place
- [ ] Code is commented where necessary
- [ ] No console.log statements in production code
- [ ] All links are valid and accessible
- [ ] Images have alt text
- [ ] Code is formatted consistently
- [ ] Version is updated if needed
- [ ] Release notes created for major changes

### AI Assistant Guidelines

When working with AI assistance:
- **Question unclear requirements** - Don't implement vague or incomplete specifications
- **Suggest improvements** - Propose better solutions when appropriate
- **Explain trade-offs** - Discuss pros and cons of different approaches
- **Validate assumptions** - Confirm understanding before implementing
- **Follow principles from "The AI-Driven Leader"** - Make informed decisions, not just execute commands

### Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- ❌ Mixing naming conventions (camelCase, snake_case, kebab-case)
- ❌ Hardcoding configuration values
- ❌ Ignoring error handling
- ❌ Committing without testing
- ❌ Leaving console.log statements
- ❌ Using inline styles (prefer CSS classes)
- ❌ Missing alt text on images
- ❌ Unvalidated user inputs
- ❌ Security vulnerabilities (XSS, hardcoded secrets)

